{"name":"CPAP (sleep apnea)","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/CPAP_continuous_positive_airway_pressure_mask_installed.jpg/960px-CPAP_continuous_positive_airway_pressure_mask_installed.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CPAP_continuous_positive_airway_pressure_mask_installed.jpg","caption":"CPAP continuous positive airway pressure mask installed","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":16,"casual":{"lead":["CPAP delivers air at constant pressure through a mask to keep your airway open during sleep. It is described as the most effective treatment for moderate to severe obstructive sleep apnea. In appearance communities, it is discussed for the fresher, less puffy look that comes with restored sleep.","Pressurised air splints the upper airway open, which eliminates obstructive apneas in most people who use it as directed, so sleep is no longer repeatedly broken. Better sleep is what users connect to less facial puffiness. In people with sleep apnea it lowers overall cardiovascular death risk; appearance outcomes have not been tested in the sources reviewed."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["CPAP is a prescription device used nightly with a nasal, full-face or hybrid mask after a sleep study diagnoses apnea. Users adjust mask type and fit with their provider. Sleep quality often changes within days to weeks of consistent use."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Adherence is a major issue: about 8% stop after the first night and more than half within a year. Reported side effects include discomfort, congestion, bloating, claustrophobia and mask leaks. It is a prescription medical device and needs a diagnosis."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/CPAP_continuous_positive_airway_pressure_mask_installed.jpg/960px-CPAP_continuous_positive_airwa